<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Merged into the generated Xcode project's Info.plist by the Tauri
       CLI. LAN discovery of the paired desktop (#511): without this key
       iOS silently drops every multicast and every connection to a
       private address, so mDNS finds nothing and pairing "just fails". -->
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Headstate Companion looks for your desktop Headstate on the local network to pair with it and send it commands.</string>
  <!-- The Bonjour service the desktop advertises (remote/discovery.rs).
       iOS refuses to browse a service type not declared here. -->
  <key>NSBonjourServiceTypes</key>
  <array>
    <string>_headstate._tcp</string>
  </array>
  <!-- Opportunistic background refresh (plugins/headstate-refresh,
       #516). BGTaskScheduler refuses to register or submit a task whose
       identifier is not listed here, and grants app-refresh windows
       only to an app that declares the `fetch` background mode. This is
       a window a few seconds long every fifteen minutes at best, not a
       stream; nothing here keeps the app alive. -->
  <key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
  <array>
    <string>com.pktstorm.headstate.companion.refresh</string>
  </array>
  <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
  <array>
    <string>fetch</string>
  </array>
  <!-- The step-up signing keys (plugins/headstate-keys) carry a
       `.userPresence` access control, so signing raises the system's
       Face ID / Touch ID / passcode sheet. Without this key iOS refuses
       Face ID for the app outright and every destructive command fails
       with an authentication error instead of a prompt. -->
  <key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Headstate Companion confirms it is you before deleting anything on your desktop.</string>
  <!-- The pairing QR is scanned with the camera (tauri-plugin-barcode-
       scanner, #514). iOS terminates an app that opens the camera
       without this string. Wording from docs/mobile-store-listings.md. -->
  <key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Headstate Companion uses the camera to scan the pairing code shown on your desktop.</string>
  <!-- Export compliance. Apple asks this of every upload; answering it
       here means a build reaches testers without someone clearing it by
       hand in App Store Connect, which is what stalled build 8.

       `true` because Apple requires documentation for an app carrying
       "standard encryption algorithms instead of, or in addition to,
       using or accessing the encryption within Apple's operating
       system", and that is exactly this app. It does not go through
       Apple's networking or CryptoKit; it links its own:

         1. TLS 1.3 with mutual authentication to the paired desktop,
            over rustls on aws-lc-rs rather than URLSession.
         2. ECDSA P-256 and ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) signatures
            authenticating commands (remote/stepup.rs), via p256 and
            ml-dsa.
         3. Stronghold, plus an AES-GCM key in the Keystore or Secure
            Enclave, protecting the app's own secrets at rest.

       Builds 8 and 9 declared `false`, on the reading that authenticating
       and protecting one's OWN data is an exempt supporting use. That
       exemption is real in the EAR, but it decides whether the software
       is CONTROLLED, a different question from whether Apple wants
       documentation. Item 3 is also confidentiality, not
       authentication. `true` is the defensible answer.

       Every algorithm above is published and standard (IETF, NIST,
       ISO), so this is NOT "non-standard cryptography": no CCATS is
       needed, only the declaration in App Store Connect. Apple issues a
       code once that is on file, which
       ITSEncryptionExportComplianceCode carries that code, and the
       release workflow INSERTS that key only when it has one to put
       there. The key is deliberately absent here: Apple validates it
       whenever it is present, and an empty string is not "no code" but a
       wrong one, which altool rejects with `Invalid Export Compliance
       Code. The export compliance key value []` (build 10).

       Separately and not satisfied by anything in this repository: US
       exporters of 5D002 software owe BIS an annual self-classification
       report, due 1 February for the preceding calendar year. See
       docs/mobile-release-process.md. -->
  <key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
